<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choe Chung-heon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1149–1219</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choe Chung-heon was a Korean general who seized power in Goryeo in 1196 and founded a military house that ruled the kingdom for four generations. He deposed two kings, ruled through his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyojeong-dogam">Directorate General for Policy Formulation</a> and private guards, and left the kings as figureheads. His descendants governed until the last Choe dictator was killed in 1258.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon.jpg" alt="Choe Chung-heon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dick Thomas Johnson from Tokyo, Japan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_National_Museum_Toyokan_(52504076964).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
